```

Grouping rule (documented at thread-mapping.ts:1-10): a consecutive run of `assistant`/`tool`/`agent` entries collapses into ONE assistant message whose `content` interleaves `text` and `tool-call` parts; `tool_result` attaches to the matching unresolved `tool-call` part by `tool_use_id` (searched backwards). Orphaned results degrade to a text part (line 116-117).

A second, unrelated dispatcher exists for the project floor tiles — `forge-control-web/app/desktop/ProjectsSurface.tsx:572-583` (`floorLine`), also keyed on `kind`/`role` only, never tool name.

---

## 3. Do Agent / SendMessage / Task / task-notification entries flow through today?

### Agent / SendMessage / Task / any MCP tool: YES, rendered — as the generic fallback

Because `thread-mapping.ts` only branches on `e.kind === "tool_call"`, an `Agent` spawn is turned into an identical `tool-call` part as a `Bash` call. `ToolCallRow` then renders it with `toolName = "Agent"` and `argsText` = the entire serialized prompt JSON (truncated to 1500 chars server-side, then to 110 chars for the collapsed preview line).

The exact branch that handles unknown/other tool names is the same branch that handles Bash — there is no discrimination. Verbatim (thread-mapping.ts:86-98):

```ts
    if (e.kind === "tool_call") {
      const meta = e.meta ?? {};
      openParts.push({
        type: "tool-call",
        toolCallId:
          typeof meta.tool_use_id === "string" ? meta.tool_use_id : `c${i}`,
        toolName:
          typeof meta.tool === "string" && meta.tool
            ? meta.tool
            : content.split(" ")[0] || "tool",
        argsText: typeof meta.input === "string" ? meta.input : content,
      });
      return;
    }
```

…and the render-side fallback registration (AssistantThread.tsx:124-129):

```tsx
        <MessagePrimitive.Parts
          components={{
            Text: AssistantText,
            tools: { Fallback: ToolCallRow },
          }}
        />
```

Live proof from the DB — tool names actually present in `runs.thread` (`meta.tool`, kind=`tool_call`, all-time counts):

```
Bash 4453 | Read 1123 | Edit 517 | WebSearch 337 | Write 205 | WebFetch 129 | Grep 99 |
Agent 63 | Glob 40 | ToolSearch 24 | TaskUpdate 22 | mcp__postgres__query 13 | TaskCreate 13 |
Skill 10 | ScheduleWakeup 4 | Monitor 3 | SendMessage 3 | CronCreate 2 | ...
```

`Task` count is 0 — the CLI names this tool **`Agent`**. `forge-control/src/routes/agents.ts:236-240` documents this explicitly:

```
// The CLI names this tool "Agent"; the docs (and older transcripts)
// call it "Task". Matching only "Task" is exactly why no sub-agent ever
// showed in the Live panel even on runs that obviously spawned them —
// this session alone logged 7 "Agent" calls and zero "Task" calls.
```

### Important side effect: subagent tool calls are inlined with no visual distinction

Subagent-produced entries carry `meta.parent_tool_use_id`. `thread-mapping.ts` **ignores `parent_tool_use_id` entirely** — so a subagent's 60 Bash calls render inline in the operator's transcript, indistinguishable from the operator's own. Only the Live panel (`app/desktop/live/AgentActivity.tsx`, backed by `GET /api/agents`, `forge-control/src/routes/agents.ts:225+` `foldSubagents` / `run-rollup.ts`) groups them.

### Task notifications: NO — they never reach the thread at all

The only tool-name dispatch in the whole system is server-side, and it is for the Live panel rollup, not the transcript. `/opt/ai-os/workspace/projects/8ea0cc08-28d9-4301-9f28-c98e1c5d6838/forge-control/src/lib/run-rollup.ts:177-181`:

```ts
      const isTaskSpawn =
        !parent &&
        typeof e.toolName === "string" &&
        (e.toolName === "Task" || e.toolName === "Agent") &&
        typeof e.toolUseId === "string";
```

